WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.020
I won the Flame Challenge!!

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:05.800
Hey Crazies.

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:08.780
In our first video about black holes, we covered the basics.

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:09.740
What are they?

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:10.880
How are they made?

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:13.380
What do we measure about them? and I said this:

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.620
They’re massive black balls of death!

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:17.260
But only if you get too close.

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:18.560
And that got me thinking:

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:20.700
What would it be like to fall into a black hole?

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:24.040
That actually depends on your point of view and the size of the black hole.

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:25.940
First, let’s revisit a couple things.

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:27.920
Black holes have two basic parts:

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:30.960
There’s a singularity where space-time curvature is infinite.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:34.540
and an event horizon where gravitational time dilation is infinite.

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:37.540
The size of that horizon is the size of the black hole,

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:39.060
which depends on its mass.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:43.880
More mass makes for a bigger black hole, but still much smaller than you’re probably thinking.

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:47.260
If you’re falling toward a black hole, the first thing you have to worry about is

00:00:47.269 --> 00:00:48.710
Tidal Forces!

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:53.649
See, the human body can only accelerate so much before bad things start to happen.

00:00:53.649 --> 00:00:55.960
We can handle a lot if it’s short and temporary.

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:59.540
Falling into a black hole can be short, but it’s nowhere near temporary.

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:02.500
Our limits for continuous exposure are much lower.

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:05.960
Somewhere between 4 and 6 Gs, you pass out.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:09.820
Somewhere between 10 and 14 Gs, you just die.

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:13.680
Somewhere between 30 and 40 Gs, you get ripped apart.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:17.320
This is why humans are a major limiting factor in space travel,

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:19.320
but it’s also an issue near black holes.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:21.780
Space-time can get really stretched.

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:24.840
If I let an expendable clone fall toward a black hole,

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:27.280
then space-time will try to stretch him too.

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:30.980
If space-time stretches enough, he’ll get ripped into a thin piece of flesh.

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:33.960
It’s called “Spaghettification” and it’s not a good time.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:37.140
But we can actually avoid it if we chose the right black hole.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.960
The bigger the black hole, the more gradual the stretch.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:44.680
100,000 solar masses should be more than enough to keep my clone conscious all the way in.

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:47.200
OK, so we’ve got a super-massive black hole to work with.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:49.800
Let’s say I’m on my space station at a safe distance.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:53.340
As the clone approaches the event horizon, he red-shifts.

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:56.980
Eventually, I have to watch him from infrared, and then microwave,

00:01:56.980 --> 00:02:01.700
and then radio, and into oblivion, but I never see him cross the horizon.

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:02.640
Ever ever?

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:03.540
Ever ever.

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:07.319
Remember, the closer you get to a source of gravity, the slower time goes.

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:09.800
It’s called “gravitational time dilation.”

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:13.000
The event horizon is a place of infinite time dilation,

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:16.320
so it would take an infinite amount of time for my clone to cross it.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:18.880
But the clone has a very different story to tell.

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:22.780
As he approaches the event horizon, he would see my time speed up,

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:26.860
What? What is that? Ah!

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:31.760
Almost anything said about black hole singularities is merely a hypothesis since

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:33.980
there has been no experimental verification of any kind.

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:37.180
Many of the details are subject to possibly significant change in the future.

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:39.300
Was that really necessary?

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:40.280
Where was I, again?

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:42.300
Oh right, toward the singularity.

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:45.620
If I’m standing in a grassy field, I could walk this way,

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:46.760
or that way,

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:47.940
or even this way,

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:50.240
all of which would take me to different places.

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:52.840
But, if this grassy field were inside of a black hole,

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:56.060
no matter which way I face, I’m facing the singularity.

00:02:56.060 --> 00:02:59.560
Of course, all the light is directed that way too so it would just look black.

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:02.800
Of course, none of the events beyond the horizon even take place in our universe,

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:04.360
so did it even happen?

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:05.520
What do you think?

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:07.680
Comment response time!

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:12.060
Kevin Suarez was amazed that LIGO had gotten switched on just in time to witness

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:13.140
the black hole merger.

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:16.260
Yes, that does seem like we’re pretty lucky, but we’re actually not.

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:20.060
With the enhanced range, we actually expected to see mergers pretty frequently.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:22.780
and, in the months between the detection and the announcement,

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:24.460
we’ve already seen several more!

00:03:24.460 --> 00:03:25.580
Links in the doobly-doo.

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:29.500
Moses asked about the number of values needed to describe space-time curvature.

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:32.100
That actually came from the Riemann curvature tensor.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:37.180
Technically, it has 256 components for 4D, but a lot of them are the same.

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:41.140
There are, at most, 20 independent components in that tensor.

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:45.460
If the space-time is simple, it could be less, but that’s super mathy stuff.

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:47.880
Speaking of mathy stuff, Matthew,

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:49.520
Eh? Eh? See what I did there?

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:50.580
I’m such a dork.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:53.580
Matthew was disappointed I didn’t go into the mathy details.

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:57.520
But to be honest, I’m usually disappointed when I have leave some of the mathy stuff out.

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:01.800
It’s tough to convey in a video without boring people to death and scaring them away.

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:04.220
I do have some good ideas on how I might make it work,

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:08.200
but that's going to take a lot more time and probably a little help to make a reality.

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:10.840
There are things that I just can’t do by myself.

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:15.260
KnowBuddies felt like I did Voltron a disservice by only showing the super-robot form.

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:16.460
You’re absolutely right.

00:04:16.460 --> 00:04:17.600
There’s no excuse.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:19.120
I stand before you in shame.

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:21.500
And Pablo, you’re very welcome for the credit.

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:22.580
It’s the least I can do.

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:24.760
That graphic of the universe is amazing.

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:25.920
Keep up the great work!

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:27.900
That’s it on gravitational waves for now.

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:31.260
Maybe this way of answering comments will stimulate some conversation.

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:33.000
Thanks for watching!

